{"hash":"0f8a969b24c4e877","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":90.364,"said":"Om Namah Shivaya. Come, walk with me into the courtyard. In front of you rises Trimbakeshwar, built of dark basalt, its spire climbing tier upon tier. Now look higher, behind it, at the long wall of Brahmagiri, the mountain where the river Godavari is born. Hold on to that mountain, because our story begins there. But first, the man who raised these stones. Balaji Bajirao, remembered as Nanasaheb, was the Peshwa, the chief minister who led the Maratha state from 1740, and the son of the great general Bajirao. The temple trust records that he built this temple on the site of an older one, and that on Mahashivratri, in February 1756, it was opened to the sound of shehnai, drums and trumpets. A story, which historians say cannot be confirmed, tells that a great diamond was set in the Lord’s crown here. What the records do show is that the last Peshwa, Bajirao the Second, had that diamond taken to Pune, and that after his defeat by the British in 1818 it went to London, where it became known as the Nassak Diamond. Now walk with me round the temple, to the old water tank.\n\nDid you know? Sant Nivruttinath, elder brother and guru of Sant Dnyaneshwar, took samadhi at Trimbakeshwar; lakhs of Varkaris gather here every year on his death anniversary.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1083},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1099,"end":1259}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,2],[0.314,0.312,3,8],[0.639,0.553,9,16],[2.127,0.457,18,22],[2.776,0.312,24,28],[3.101,0.204,29,33],[3.317,0.168,34,36],[3.498,0.24,37,41],[3.75,0.084,42,45],[3.846,0.613,46,55],[5.361,0.156,57,59],[5.529,0.312,60,65],[5.853,0.168,66,68],[6.034,0.144,69,72],[6.19,0.481,73,78],[6.683,0.877,79,92],[7.788,0.276,94,99],[8.077,0.132,100,102],[8.221,0.312,103,107],[8.546,0.565,108,114],[9.375,0.168,116,119],[9.555,0.361,120,125],[9.928,0.517,126,134],[10.457,0.337,135,139],[10.805,0.349,140,144],[11.166,0.385,145,149],[